Me: I am a computer science student and for my final project I have to develop a web application with python and Drafter. It has to have at least 7 routes, 5 pages, a State dataclass with at least 4 fields (with at least one field being a list of class instances or a dictionary of class instances, and the fields need to be meaningfully modified in at least one route, they can't be constants), a second dataclass, at least 3 input fields, at least 3 meaningful if statements, at least 1 loop that iterates over an attribute of the list of class instances or dictionary of class instances in a meaningful way, and no global variables. The project I am moving forward with is a "What One Piece Character Are You?" quiz, where based off the answers the user gives to the questions, it will show what One Piece character they relate with the most. I am entirely allowed (and encouraged) to use AI for this project.
After doing some planning, I have come up with this plan for the website.
Five State attributes:
user_name (str): Stores the user's name. Modified when the user enters their name on the home page.
current_page (str): Tracks which page the user is currently viewing. Modified when they navigate between pages.
quiz_answers (list[str]): Stores the user's answers to the quiz questions. New answers are added when the user submits their quiz.
characters (list[Character]): Stores instances of the Character dataclass. Character scores are updated based on the user's answers.
result (str): Stores the name of the One Piece character that best matches the user's answers. Modified after the quiz is completed.
Second dataclass (Character):
name (str): Stores the character's name.
description (str): Stores a short personality description.
traits (list[str]): Stores personality traits associated with the character.
score (int): Tracks how many points the character receives from the user's answers. Modified throughout quiz evaluation.
For the Character dataclass, start with these 5 characters that could end up being the quiz result.
-Monkey D. Luffy
-Roronoa Zoro
-Vinsmoke Sanji
-Nami
-Tony Tony Chopper
routes:
home: Displays the welcome page and allows users to enter their name.
instructions: Explains how the personality quiz works and what users should expect.
quiz: Displays the personality questions and input fields (questions should be multiple choice).
submit_quiz: Processes the user's answers and calculates character scores. (this is a processing route, not a separate page.)
results: Displays the character that best matches the user's personality.
character_directory: Displays all five One Piece characters and their descriptions.
character_details: Displays more information about a selected character from the character directory when they are clicked on.
restart: Resets the quiz so the user can take it again.
Pages:
home page: First page users will see. Has a the title and includes a TextBox where the user can enter their name. Also has a button that will take the user to the character directory page when clicked.
instructions page: Explains the purpose of the quiz, how the quiz will work, how many questions there are. A start button will take the user to the quiz page when clicked.
quiz page: Displays the five quiz personality questions. User selects their answers using SelectBox inputs (all questions are multiple choice). When they have answered all questions, there is a submit button that takes them to the results page when clicked.
results page: Displays the character that best matches the user's personality. Shows the character's name, image, description, and matching personality traits. Also has a restart button that takes the user back to the home screen when clicked and allows them to retake the quiz.
character directory page: Displays the five characters included in the quiz. Users can browse their names, images, and descriptions.
character details page: Displays more detailed information about the selected character from the character directory page, including their personality traits and image.
User inputs:
TextBox: Input the user's name on the home page.
SelectBox (for questions 1,2,3,4,5): User clicks on the button to select their answer for a given question.
Meaningful if statements:
If statement 1: Checking whether the user entered their name
When the user clicks the start button, the program checks whether the name field is empty.
If the user entered a name, they can proceed to the instructions page.
Otherwise, the application displays a message asking them to enter their name.
If statement 2: Checking whether all quiz questions have been answered
When the user submits the quiz, the program checks whether they answered all three questions.
If all questions have answers, the application calculates their character match.
Otherwise, the application displays a message asking them to complete the unanswered questions.
If statement 3: Determining the character with the highest score
After calculating the scores, the program checks which character has the highest score.
If a character has a higher score than the current best match, that character becomes the new result.
This ensures the application displays the character who received the most points.
If statement 4: Checking whether the user wants to restart
When the user clicks the restart button, the program resets their answers and character scores.
The user is returned to the quiz's starting page.
This allows them to take the quiz again without their previous results affecting the new quiz.
1 meaningful loop:
The loop will iterate through the characters list in the State dataclass to determine which One Piece character best matches the user's answers.
Here is how it will work:
When the user submits their quiz, the program retrieves their answers.
The program loops through every Character instance stored in state.characters.
For each character, the program compares their personality traits with the traits associated with the user's answers.
Every time a character matches an answer, their score increases.
After checking all five characters, the program identifies which character has the highest score.
The winning character's name is stored in state.result.
